add(dict(t="title",text="What 311 Data Can and Cannot Tell Us: Analytical Choices and Equity Conclusions in Municipal Service Research"))
add(dict(t="center",text="[Author Name]")); add(dict(t="center",text="[Affiliation] · [Email]")); add(dict(t="center",text="Manuscript draft, October 2026",size=20))
add(dict(t="h1",text="Abstract"))
add(dict(t="abstract",text=f"Municipal 311 data are now a standard source for studying whether public services reach disadvantaged neighborhoods, but researchers choose which requests to keep, which outcome to measure, which controls to add and which neighborhood measure to use, often without reporting the alternatives. Using 4.2 million resident requests to Chicago’s 311 system (2019–2024), I estimate {SM['n_specs']:,} analyses that cross five sample definitions, six time windows, three control sets, four outcome definitions and three neighborhood measures, and report how each choice moves the estimated hardship gradient in completion time. The gradient is positive in {SM['hardship_share_positive']*100:.0f} percent of the {SM['hardship_specs']} analyses that use the Hardship Index (though close to zero with request-type controls and the days outcome), and significant at 5 percent with community-area clustering in {pct_sig:.0f} percent; ordinary standard errors would call {pct_hc1:.0f} percent significant. Estimates range from {SM['hardship_std_min']:.2f} to {SM['hardship_std_max']:.2f} standard deviations of outcome. Records, controls and outcome definition each explain about a tenth of that variation, while the time window explains about one percent. A winter-avoidance account of the hardship gradient is not supported: the gradient is no larger in winter than in other seasons, and after controlling for type-by-season composition it is smaller, while violent crime does not change this pattern. The paper argues for reporting a grid of analyses, clustering at the level of the treatment, and separating data-construction choices from substantive claims."))
add(dict(t="p",text="<b>Keywords:</b> 311 systems; administrative data; analytical flexibility; multiverse analysis; specification curves; urban service equity; Chicago"))
add(dict(t="h1",text="1. Introduction"))
add(dict(t="p",text="A 311 request is an unusually rich record: it carries a time stamp for creation and closure, a location, a request type, and a channel through which it arrived. Those features have made 311 data the basis for a growing literature on whether urban services are distributed fairly across neighborhoods. Yet a raw download is not a measure of service delivery. It mixes resident requests with work that crews generate for themselves, with information-only calls that close in seconds, and with duplicate reports that close when their parent closes. Each of these records can be kept or dropped, and each choice can change the answer. Before any substantive claim about equity can be made, a researcher must also choose an outcome, a set of controls, a window of time and a neighborhood measure."))
add(dict(t="p",text="These choices are rarely pre-specified and seldom reported as alternatives. When they are, the results can differ sharply. The companion studies of Chicago’s 311 system report that requests from high-hardship community areas wait longer than otherwise identical requests elsewhere ([Author], 2026a), and that the racial gradient is stronger than the economic one at tract level ([Author], 2026b). Those conclusions rest on specific choices, and a reader cannot tell from either paper how much of the result each choice produces. This article makes that question the object of study."))
add(dict(t="p",text="Three questions organize the paper. First, how much does a Chicago 311 equity estimate depend on the analytical choices a researcher makes? Second, which choices matter most? Third, does the evidence support a substantive mechanism that many readers would find plausible, namely that the lag in service to high-hardship areas grows in Chicago winters, when crews may avoid those areas, and grows further where violent crime is high? The third question is a test of whether a seasonal or crime-based pattern can be read from the data at all."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The answers are as follows. The hardship gradient is positive in most analyses, but its size varies by a factor of several, and the choice of records, controls and outcome account for most of that variation. Clustering matters as much as the sample: conventional standard errors make {pct_hc1-pct_sig:.0f} percentage points more of the analyses significant. The seasonal hypothesis is not supported. The gradient does not widen in winter, and crime does not account for any seasonal change. The paper proposes a reporting standard for 311 equity studies built on these findings, and it draws a clear line between estimates that are robust across the grid and those that depend on one defensible-looking choice."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="2. Background: analytical flexibility in administrative data"))
add(dict(t="p",text="Analytical flexibility is a long-standing concern in empirical research. Simmons, Nelson and Simonsohn (2011) showed how undisclosed choices in data collection and analysis can make almost any result appear significant, and Steegen, Tuerlinckx, Gelman and Vanpaemel (2016) proposed a multiverse analysis that reports the estimate under every defensible combination of choices. Simonsohn, Simmons and Nelson (2020) formalized the approach as the specification curve: identify the set of theoretically justified, statistically valid and non-redundant specifications; display the estimates graphically; and conduct joint inference across them. The approach has been applied mainly to experimental and survey data in psychology."))
add(dict(t="p",text="Administrative data raise the same issue in a more pronounced form, because the cleaning stage is larger and less visible. A 311 export is not a sample drawn for a purpose; it is a record of every interaction with the system, including interactions that are not service requests at all. Researchers therefore face a sequence of decisions before any model is estimated, and the same decisions are repeated across studies with different choices. Within the 311 literature, Minkoff (2016) studied the spatial distribution of contacts with New York City government, Clark, Brudney and Jang (2013, 2020) compared response across neighborhoods and cities, and White and Trump (2018) warned against reading 311 call rates as generic measures of civic engagement. Those studies differ in exactly the choices examined here, which makes their disagreements hard to interpret."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="3. Data and the baseline replication"))
add(dict(t="p",text="The data are the City of Chicago’s 311 Service Requests for 2019–2024, the Crimes extracts, the Building Violations extracts, the 2008–2012 Hardship Index for community areas and the 2023 ACS community-area profile. The request file, exclusions and outcome definitions are those of the companion studies: information-only calls and aircraft-noise complaints are excluded, the analysis covers 49 physical-service request types, and the outcome is log(1 + hours to completion), winsorized at the type-specific 99th percentile. The baseline sample contains 3,540,652 completed, non-duplicate resident requests."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"Community-area measures were rebuilt from the raw files. The Hardship Index has a mean of 49.5 and a standard deviation of 28.7; violent-crime and building-violation rates reproduce the companion paper’s quartile means to within 0.1 point; and the correlations of hardship with the Black share (0.47) and of the low-income share with the Black share (0.74) match. Table 1 reproduces the companion paper’s main estimates on the same data. Every coefficient and standard error matches to the third decimal, which establishes that the analysis file and estimator are correct before any alternative is run."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 1. Reproduction of the companion paper’s estimates (Chicago 311, 2019–2024)",cols=[4300,2300,2400],header=["Specification","Companion paper","Rebuilt here"],rows=REP,align=["l","c","c"],note="Speed sample of 3,540,652 completed, non-duplicate resident requests unless noted; 77 community-area clusters; standard errors in parentheses. Hardship is standardized across community areas. The unresolved-share estimate uses the 3,677,012 resident non-duplicate requests. Rebuilt values are computed by the replication script p3_repro.py."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="4. Analytical choices"))
add(dict(t="p",text="I vary five dimensions. Each is a choice a researcher could defend, and each appears in published 311 research. Table 2 lists them. The grid is not exhaustive: it omits choices about the spatial unit, the form of winsorization, and the treatment of open requests, which are discussed in Section 7. Every combination of the five dimensions yields one analysis, for a total of 1,080."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 2. Analytical choices in the multiverse",cols=[1700,4200,3100],header=["Dimension","Levels","What the choice means"],rows=[
 ["Records","Resident non-duplicate (baseline); + duplicates; + city-generated; + information-only calls; all three","Whether requests that are duplicates, crew-generated or information-only calls are kept in the speed analysis"],
 ["Window","2019–2024; excluding 2020; 2019 only; 2022–2024; winter (Dec–Feb); summer (Jun–Aug)","Which years and seasons are analyzed"],
 ["Controls","None; request type × year; full (type × year, month, weekday, hour, channel, workload)","How much request composition is held fixed"],
 ["Outcome","log(1 + hours); percentile rank within type-year; slower than type-year median; days","How completion time is scaled"],
 ["Neighborhood measure","Hardship Index; share of households under $25,000; share Black residents","Which community-area characteristic is the regressor (each standardized)"]],align=["l","l","l"],note="Clustering is at the community-area level (77 clusters) in every analysis unless stated. Ward clustering and heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors are reported in Section 5.3."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="5. Results"))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.1 The distribution of estimates"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"Figure 1 shows all {SM['hardship_specs']} analyses that use the Hardship Index, sorted by their standardized estimate (the coefficient divided by the standard deviation of the outcome, which makes the four outcome definitions comparable). The estimates have a median of {SM['hardship_std_median']:.3f} standard deviations of outcome and range from {SM['hardship_std_min']:.3f} to {SM['hardship_std_max']:.3f}. The gradient is positive in {SM['hardship_share_positive']*100:.0f} percent of analyses and significant in {pct_sig:.0f} percent. The {n_nonpos} analyses with a zero or negative gradient are concentrated in a few places: 23 use no controls at all, and nine use the 2019-only window, which has the smallest sample. Without controls for request type, the gradient partly reflects which services high-hardship areas request, rather than how fast they are served. The three measures tell the same qualitative story: {SM['low_inc_share_pos_sig']*100:.0f} percent of low-income-share analyses and {SM['pct_black_share_pos_sig']*100:.0f} percent of Black-share analyses are positive and significant."))
add(img:=dict(t="figure",path="figs/fig_specurve.png",w=fs[0],h=fs[1],width_in=6.0,caption="Figure 1. Specification curve for the Hardship Index gradient",note="Each point is one analysis (360 in total), sorted by the coefficient divided by the standard deviation of the outcome. Red points are significant at 5 percent with community-area clustering. The lower panel marks each analysis’s choices: an analysis contains one mark in each record, window, control and outcome row that applies to it."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.2 Which choices matter"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"Table 3 shows the mean standardized estimate and its range for each level of each dimension. Two patterns stand out. First, the records decision matters: keeping information-only calls raises the average gradient to {CH['records']['+ information-only calls']['mean']:.3f}, and keeping all records to {CH['records']['+ all three']['mean']:.3f}, against {CH['records']['Resident, non-duplicate (baseline)']['mean']:.3f} for the baseline. Information-only calls are closed almost at once, so keeping them shifts the completion-time distribution toward instant closures, and their spatial pattern affects which places look slow. Second, the controls and outcome decisions matter: with no controls the average gradient is {CH['controls']['none']['mean']:.3f}, against {CH['controls']['ty']['mean']:.3f} with request type by year fixed effects, and the log outcome gives a larger average ({CH['outcome']['log']['mean']:.3f}) than the rank outcome ({CH['outcome']['rank']['mean']:.3f}). The window moves the average less than records or controls do, but not negligibly: the mean runs from {min(CH['window'][w]['mean'] for w in CH['window']):.3f} (2019 only) to {max(CH['window'][w]['mean'] for w in CH['window']):.3f} (2022–2024)."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"Taken together, the four dimensions that vary within the Hardship Index analyses (the neighborhood measure is fixed) explain {SM['r2_choices_hardship']*100:.0f} percent of the variation in standardized estimates. Records, controls and outcome each explain roughly a tenth of it ({SM['choice_r2_by_dim']['records']*100:.0f}, {SM['choice_r2_by_dim']['controls']*100:.0f} and {SM['choice_r2_by_dim']['outcome']*100:.0f} percent alone), and the window explains {SM['choice_r2_by_dim']['window']*100:.0f} percent. A reader who wants to know whether a result depends on the period studied can be reassured; a reader who wants to know whether it depends on what is counted, how it is scaled, or what else is held fixed should look harder."))
rows3=[]
for key,title,*lv in DIMS:
    rows3.append([title]); 
    for l in lv: v=CH[key][l]; rows3.append([f"   {LAB.get(l,l)}",f"{v['mean']:.3f}",f"{v['min']:.3f} to {v['max']:.3f}",str(v['n'])])
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 3. Hardship gradient by analytical choice (standardized, hardship specifications)",cols=[4000,1600,2300,1100],header=["Choice","Mean","Range across other choices","Analyses"],rows=rows3,align=["l","c","c","c"],note="Standardized gradient = coefficient / standard deviation of the outcome, for the 360 Hardship Index analyses. Each mean averages the analyses that share that level of the choice."))
add(dict(t="figure",path="figs/fig_choices.png",w=fc[0],h=fc[1],width_in=5.6,caption="Figure 2. Contribution of each choice to the hardship gradient",note="Dots are the mean standardized gradient at each level of each choice; bars give its range across the other choices. Source: Table 3."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.3 Clustering can decide significance"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The estimates are based on 77 community areas, which is few for cluster-robust inference and many for the request-level model. Standard errors that treat each request as independent make {pct_hc1:.0f} percent of Hardship Index analyses significant, against {pct_sig:.0f} percent with community-area clustering, and {(((H.p_hc1<0.05)&(H.p_ca>=0.05)).mean()*100):.0f} percent of analyses are significant only under the independence assumption. A paper that reports only the former would overstate the precision of a neighborhood-level estimate by a large margin. Clustering at the community area is the appropriate unit here, because the hardship measure varies only at that level; clustering by ward, which the companion papers also report, gives a similar share of significant analyses ({WARD_SIG:.0f} percent against {CA_SIG:.0f} percent with community-area clusters)."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.4 Did the gradient widen in winter, when crews might avoid high-hardship areas?"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The question has an intuitive appeal. Winter weather, short days and crew scheduling could make it harder to reach high-hardship neighborhoods, and violent crime, which is concentrated in those neighborhoods, could make crews more reluctant to visit. If so, the hardship gradient should be larger in winter, and larger still where crime is high. The data can test the first part of this account, because completion times and request dates are recorded for every request. They cannot observe crew decisions, so the test below is a test of the pattern such a mechanism would produce, not of the mechanism itself."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The data do not show a winter widening. The hardship gradient is {A['H_winter'][0]:.3f} log points per standard deviation in winter, {A['H_summer'][0]:.3f} in summer, and between {A['H_fall'][0]:.3f} and {A['H_spring'][0]:.3f} in the other seasons. Winter minus the other seasons is {A['winter_minus_others'][0]:.3f} (SE {A['winter_minus_others'][1]:.3f}), which is zero within sampling error. Controlling for request composition by type, year and season makes the winter gradient smaller than the others ({B['winter_minus_others'][0]:.3f}, SE {B['winter_minus_others'][1]:.3f}), the opposite of the hypothesis. Month by month, the gradient is lowest in April and highest in June ({mon[5]:.3f}), with December at {mon[11]:.3f} and January at {mon[0]:.3f}. Adding violent crime interacted with season, which the crime hypothesis requires, leaves the winter-minus-other-seasons hardship difference at {X['winter_minus_others_H'][0]:.3f} (SE {X['winter_minus_others_H'][1]:.3f}) and the crime difference at {X['winter_minus_others_V'][0]:.3f} (SE {X['winter_minus_others_V'][1]:.3f}). Neither is distinguishable from zero."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The aggregate hides different departmental patterns (Table 4). The gradient in Streets and Sanitation, the department with the most requests, does not change with season. In Transportation it is markedly smaller in winter ({CD['winter_minus_others'][0]:.3f}), and in Water Management it is larger ({WM['winter_minus_others'][0]:.3f}). A crew-avoidance account would predict a winter widening in the departments whose crews move through neighborhoods, and the Transportation result runs the other way. The Water Management result is consistent with the hypothesis but rests on a smaller department; I did not estimate it with the type-by-season composition control."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 4. Does the hardship gradient change with season or with violent crime?",cols=[5300,2000,1700],header=["","Coefficient (SE)","Note"],rows=[[r[0],r[1],""] if len(r)==4 else r for r in ref_rows()],align=["l","c","c"],note="Standardized hardship gradients (log points per SD) estimated from the baseline sample with request type × year, month, weekday, hour and channel fixed effects and log workload, clustered by community area. Season-specific gradients come from interacting the hardship measure with season indicators. Panel B row 3 adds violent-crime rate interacted with season; row 4 reports its winter-minus-other-seasons difference. Type × year × season FE absorb composition that varies by season. Panel C: departmental analogs of Panel B, first row."))
mo=pd.read_csv("results/A3_season_composition.csv",index_col=0)
_fm=Image.open("figs/fig_monthly.png").size
add(dict(t="figure",path="figs/fig_monthly.png",w=_fm[0],h=_fm[1],width_in=5.6,caption="Figure 3. Monthly hardship gradient in completion time",note="Each point is the hardship gradient (log points per SD) for requests created in that calendar month, from the baseline sample with request type × year, weekday, hour, channel and workload controls and month-of-sample fixed effects; bars are 95 percent intervals clustered by community area. Shaded: winter months."))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"A second reason the composition control matters is that snow-related requests are almost entirely a winter phenomenon. Of winter requests, {mo.loc['Winter','snow_share']:.1f} percent are snow-related types (ice and snow removal, snow object removal, uncleared sidewalks), against {mo.loc['Spring','snow_share']:.1f}, {mo.loc['Summer','snow_share']:.2f} and {mo.loc['Fall','snow_share']:.1f} percent in the other seasons. A comparison of winter with other seasons that does not hold request type fixed therefore compares different service mixes. The share of requests from high-hardship areas is nearly constant across seasons (between {mo['hi_share'].min():.1f} and {mo['hi_share'].max():.1f} percent), so the seasonal comparison is not confounded by where requests come from, only by what they are."))
add(dict(t="p",text="Two cautions apply. Season changes the request mix: snow-related request types appear only in winter, and some types are more common in summer. The type-by-season composition control addresses this, but the result is sensitive to it, which is itself informative: the seasonal story depends on how the mix is handled. Second, crime is measured as a community-area annual rate, so it cannot capture the within-year timing that a crime-avoidance mechanism would require. A finer measure, such as weekly crime by community area, would be needed for that test."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.5 Is the cleaned sample the same as the raw one?"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The choices above are where a researcher’s judgment enters the estimate. Several are not judgment calls at all, but corrections that remove mechanical bias. Keeping city-generated records, which crews open and close in the same minute, lowers the average standardized gradient slightly (from 0.022 to 0.016 in Table 3), while keeping information-only calls raises it to 0.088, because those calls close almost at once and are not evenly spread across the city. The lesson is not that cleaning is arbitrary, but that the cleaning rules must be stated and tested rather than assumed."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.6 Which of the companion paper’s claims survive the grid?"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"The companion study reports a hardship gradient of 8.1 percent per standard deviation under its baseline choices. Table 5 asks which of its claims hold across the grid. The sign and existence of the gradient survive in most analyses. Its size does not: with request-type controls the standardized gradient lies between {TYP_MIN:.3f} and {TYP_MAX:.3f} standard deviations across record definitions, windows and outcomes. The baseline value (0.035) sits in the upper part of the log-outcome range (0.000 to 0.046), and under the days outcome the typed-control gradient is close to zero. Without request-type controls the gradient reaches {NOC_MAX:.3f}. The claim that the gradient is significant at the community-area level holds in {pct_sig:.0f} percent of analyses, and the claim that it is significant when requests are treated as independent holds in {pct_hc1:.0f} percent."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 5. Robustness of the companion paper’s claims across the multiverse",cols=[3300,2300,3400],header=["Claim","Companion paper","Across the grid"],rows=[
 ["Hardship predicts slower completion","Yes; 8.1 percent per SD (log points 0.081)",f"Positive in {SM['hardship_share_positive']*100:.0f} percent of Hardship analyses"],
 ["Size of the gradient (baseline)","0.035 SD of outcome (0.081 log points)",f"Typed controls: {TYP_MIN:.3f} to {TYP_MAX:.3f} SD across outcomes (days outcome near zero); no type controls: up to {NOC_MAX:.3f} SD"],
 ["Gradient is significant","Community-area clusters",f"Significant in {pct_sig:.0f} percent; {pct_hc1:.0f} percent under independent errors"],
 ["Gradient is stable over time","Year-by-year pattern shown (2019–2024)","Window changes the mean standardized gradient by at most 0.03 SD (Table 3)"],
 ["Records choice does not matter","Baseline definitions used",f"Records change the estimate with no type controls (up to {NOC_MAX:.3f}); with type controls, {TYP_MIN:.3f} to {TYP_MAX:.3f}"]],align=["l","l","l"],note="Standardized gradients are coefficients divided by the standard deviation of the outcome, so 0.035 corresponds to the companion paper’s 8.1 percent (0.081 log points). ‘Typed controls’ means request type × year fixed effects are included. Values come from the 1,080 analyses in the grid; the year-by-year pattern is reported in the companion paper, not repeated here."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 6. A reporting checklist for 311 equity estimates",cols=[2300,3700,3000],header=["Element","What to report","Example from this paper"],rows=[
 ["Record definitions","The rules for keeping or dropping duplicates, city-generated records and information-only calls, with counts for each","Keeping information-only calls moves the average gradient from 0.022 to 0.088 SD (Table 3)"],
 ["Controls","The fixed effects and workload terms, and an analysis without request-type controls","Without type controls the gradient reaches 0.58 SD for one record definition (Table A2)"],
 ["Outcome scale","The outcome in the units used, and a standardized version","Standardizing puts the four outcome definitions on a common scale (Figure 1)"],
 ["Window","Results by year and by season, so the reader can see where the pattern is driven","The window moves the average by up to 0.03 SD, less than records or controls"],
 ["Inference","Standard errors clustered at the level of the measure, and under independence for comparison","Clustering changes significance in about 18 percent of analyses"],
 ["Mechanism tests","Seasonal and crime interactions with request-type composition held fixed, and the limits of the data for crew-level mechanisms","The winter widening disappears once composition is held fixed (Table 4)"]],align=["l","l","l"],note="The checklist applies to any 311 analysis that compares neighborhoods on completion time. Items should be reported whether or not they change the substantive conclusion."))
add(dict(t="h2",text="5.7 Four defensible analysts, four answers"))
def _pick(rec,win,ctl,out):
    r=H[(H.records==rec)&(H.window==win)&(H.controls==ctl)&(H.outcome==out)].iloc[0]; return r["std"],r.p_ca
PATHS=[("A. Keeps every record; no controls; log hours","+ all three","2019-2024","none","log"),
       ("B. Baseline records; request-type × year controls; log hours","Resident, non-duplicate (baseline)","2019-2024","ty","log"),
       ("C. Baseline records; full controls; days to completion","Resident, non-duplicate (baseline)","2019-2024","full","days"),
       ("D. Baseline records; full controls; winter only","Resident, non-duplicate (baseline)","Winter (Dec-Feb)","full","log")]
prow=[]
for lab,rec,win,ctl,out in PATHS:
    v,p=_pick(rec,win,ctl,out); prow.append([lab,f"{v:.3f}",f"{p:.3f}"])
add(dict(t="p",text="To see what the choices mean in practice, consider four analysts who each make a defensible set of decisions and report the result (Table 5a). Each path is in the grid, and each would pass referee scrutiny on its own terms. Analyst A keeps every record, which is a legitimate reading of ‘all requests’, and omits controls, which some studies do. Analyst B uses the baseline records and holds request type fixed, as the companion paper does. Analyst C measures completion in days, which is a natural unit for a city manager. Analyst D restricts the sample to winter, as the seasonal hypothesis suggests. Their estimates differ by more than an order of magnitude, and only Analyst B’s model holds the service mix fixed."))
add(dict(t="table",title="Table 5a. Four defensible paths to a hardship gradient",cols=[5000,2000,2000],header=["Path (choices)","Standardized gradient (SD of outcome)","p (community-area clusters)"],rows=prow,align=["l","c","c"],note="Each row is one analysis from the 1,080-analysis grid (hardship measure). Analyst A’s estimate is high in part because information-only calls are included and no request-type controls are used (Table A2). Analyst C’s small value reflects the days outcome, whose standard deviation (133 days) is about three times its mean (42 days), which shrinks the standardized coefficient. Analyst D’s winter-only estimate (0.025 SD, about 0.06 log points) differs from the season-interaction estimate in Table 4 (0.077 log points) because the two models compare winter with other months in different ways; the grid reports the winter-only estimate, and Table 4 reports the interaction. The rows are examples chosen to illustrate the spread, not the full distribution."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="6. Discussion"))
add(dict(t="p",text="The central result is that a Chicago 311 equity estimate is a property of an analysis, not only of the data. The same records, measured with defensible choices, produce gradients that are positive and significant in most cases and that vary by a factor of several. What is robust across the grid is that the hardship gradient is usually positive; it is close to zero under the days outcome with request-type controls. What is not robust is its size, and the choices that change its size are the ones that decide what is counted and how it is scaled, rather than the period studied."))
add(dict(t="p",text="For practice, I suggest four rules. Report a grid of analyses, not one estimate, with the baseline clearly identified, and show it in a specification curve. Separate data-construction decisions (what counts as a request) from substantive decisions (what is held fixed and what is compared), and report the first before the second. Cluster at the level at which the treatment varies, and report the result under conventional errors as well, so that readers can see how much significance depends on the choice. And treat seasonal and crime-based mechanisms as hypotheses to be tested with the appropriate resolution, not as patterns to be read from annual or aggregate data."))
add(dict(t="p",text="The winter question shows the limits of the data. A 311 record gives the time a request was made and the time it was closed, but not the crew’s route, the work order sequence, or the reason a job waited. A mechanism that operates through those features cannot be observed directly from the records, and an aggregate seasonal pattern that does not appear cannot prove the mechanism absent. The honest conclusion is that the data are consistent with no winter widening, and that a test requires crew-level data."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="7. Limitations"))
add(dict(t="p",text="The grid is large but not exhaustive. It omits the spatial unit (community area rather than tract, which the race-and-class study uses), the form of winsorization, the treatment of open requests (dropped rather than censored, as in the companion studies), and the set of neighborhood measures beyond three. Some of these choices would shift the estimates further. The analyses are associational: hardship is not randomly assigned, and no result here identifies an effect of neighborhood conditions on service. The community-area sample has 77 units, so the estimates for any one measure rest on limited independent variation, and the clustering choice matters for that reason. The violent-crime measure is an annual community-area rate, and the crime and building-violation counts come from the public extracts. Building-violation counts match the companion paper exactly, while the unique crime incidents (1,447,178) differ slightly from the companion count (1,447,790) because one uploaded extract is missing. The seasonal analysis cannot observe crew behavior, and its composition controls are imperfect. Finally, this is a study of one city over one period, with a 311 system whose coding changed during the period."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="8. Conclusion"))
add(dict(t="p",text=f"Chicago’s 311 data support the conclusion that requests from high-hardship neighborhoods are completed more slowly, but the size of that gradient depends on what is counted, what is held fixed and how completion time is scaled, far more than on which years are studied. The winter-avoidance hypothesis is not supported: the gradient does not widen in winter, and crime does not change that pattern. A reporting standard that presents the grid, separates construction from comparison, and clusters appropriately would make 311 equity claims both more credible and more useful to the cities that rely on them."))
add(dict(t="h1",text="Declarations"))
add(dict(t="p",text="<b>Data availability.</b> All data are public: City of Chicago Data Portal (311 Service Requests; Crimes – 2001 to Present; Building Violations; Boundaries – Community Areas), Census Data – Selected Socioeconomic Indicators in Chicago, 2008–2012, and 2023 ACS community-area profile. <b>Code availability.</b> Replication code will be deposited in a public repository [link to be added]. <b>Funding.</b> [None / details.] <b>Competing interests.</b> [None.]"))
